WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.133
You already own one of the most valuable assets in the age of artificial

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.060
intelligence.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.980
You built it visit by visit, record by record, over years of real work.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.915
This video shows you what it is worth, why the buyers need it now, and how to lease

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.520
it without ever selling it.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.080
Every organization counts what it owns.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:26.640
The building, the equipment, the receivables, but almost nobody counts the records.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.540
Every intake form, every clinical note, every service call, every transaction, every

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.560
outcome, in the order it actually happened.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.960
That history, your history, is data.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.614
And right now, it is the one asset the largest technology companies on earth cannot

00:00:43.614 --> 00:00:47.360
manufacture, cannot scrape, and cannot buy off a shelf.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.160
Here is what we will cover.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.560
Why the public data the AI industry was built on has run out.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.280
What private industry data is, and why it is different.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.520
What records like yours are worth.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.120
Why you lease your data and never sell it.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.360
How you are protected at every step.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.840
And the five steps from where you sit today to getting paid.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.160
The first generation of artificial intelligence was trained on the public internet.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.840
Everything that was ever posted, published, or uploaded for anyone to see.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.840
That well has been drawn dry.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.840
The models have read it all many times over.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.360
Re-reading the same public text no longer makes them smarter.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.040
So the frontier moved.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.240
The gains now come from data that was never public.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.600
The records of real work in real industries produced by real professionals.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.880
How a patient actually presented.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.040
What the clinician actually did.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.980
What actually happened next.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.874
That knowledge exists in only one place, inside the systems of the organizations

00:01:49.874 --> 00:01:51.200
that did the work.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.376
That is why AI laboratories and technology buyers are now coming to private

00:01:56.376 --> 00:02:01.760
industry, healthcare first, and every sector behind it, and paying for access.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.000
Public data is exhausted.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Private data is the frontier.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.040
And private data belongs to its owners, people like you.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.200
Private industry data is not surveys, and it is not opinions.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.000
It is the working record of real operations.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.224
Written at the point of work — decisions made in sequence, outcomes measured over

00:02:23.224 --> 00:02:24.320
months and years.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.560
It teaches an AI system the things the public internet cannot.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.125
How experts actually write, decide, and follow through in the real world with real

00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:34.600
stakes.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.914
In healthcare, it is clinical documentation, including records written natively in

00:02:39.914 --> 00:02:44.320
English and Spanish and Arabic among the scarcest data in the world.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.760
In logistics, it is routing and delivery history.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.200
In manufacturing, production and quality records.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.440
In professional services, matters handled and results achieved.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.360
In a non-profit, years of program and service records.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.560
Different industries, one principle.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.800
Real work recorded that was never published.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.680
Scarcity is exactly what makes it valuable.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.800
What is a body of records worth?

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:16.573
On record, licensing engagements for real world provider data run from $100,000 to

00:03:16.573 --> 00:03:18.720
$2 million per engagement.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.920
Where a given organization lands depends on drivers you can count.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.744
How many records, how many years of history, how complete and structured the

00:03:27.744 --> 00:03:32.885
documentation is, which languages it covers, and whether outcomes can be followed

00:03:32.885 --> 00:03:33.520
over time.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.000
Two things matter here.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.720
First, the value is real, and it is being paid today.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.560
Second, your records do not earn anything sitting in your system.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.105
The same history that costs you money to store is the history a buyer will pay to

00:03:48.105 --> 00:03:52.280
license if it is evaluated, packaged, and protected properly.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.320
Now the most important part.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.080
You do not sell your data.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.920
You lease it, the legal term is licensing.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.760
A license is permission, a defined buyer, a defined use, a defined period.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.320
Ownership never leaves you.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.320
Your records stay yours in your systems under your control.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.412
Because it is a license and not a sale, the same body of records can be licensed

00:04:15.412 --> 00:04:19.040
again to another buyer for another use in another period.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.280
One asset earning more than once, and the economics are set before anything moves.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.706
Of every license value, 80%

00:04:28.706 --> 00:04:32.640
goes to you, the owner.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.800
Protection is not a promise at the end of this process.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.920
It is the structure of the process itself.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.720
The evaluation counts.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.240
It does not read.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.431
It works from numbers, how many records, how many years, which systems, never from

00:04:47.431 --> 00:04:48.760
the underlying files.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.600
Not patient records, not client files, not case data.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.800
Before any buyer sees anything beyond counts, agreements are signed.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.157
Confidentiality first, then the licensing agreement that fixes your share, your

00:05:02.157 --> 00:05:03.920
rights, and your approval.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.250
When a license proceeds, the data is de-identified by certified experts, prepared

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.646
inside your own systems under those agreements, and an owner is introduced only to

00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:16.160
the buyers they choose.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.840
At no point does anyone need you to hand over your records and hope.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:23.400
You approve every step in writing before it happens.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.680
Why now?

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.760
Because the buyers are building their source networks today.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.893
